WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.090 --> 00:00:05.740
More than ten years after going off the air,
Chappelle's Show is still eminently watchable,

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:07.580
quotable, and hilarious.

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:10.380
But why did Dave Chappelle resonate in the
first place?

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:14.480
And why, after all these years, do we still
need him more than ever?

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:15.620
High And Low

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:20.539
Has anybody so enthusiastic about diarrhea
humor ever been called a "genius" as often

00:00:20.539 --> 00:00:21.830
as Dave Chappelle?

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:25.910
In a single stand-up set, he can go from mudbutt
into dangerous neighborhoods, highlight the

00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:30.160
injustice of our legal system, incisively
criticize the president, and then leave the

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:34.260
planet with three cloned triplets, a space
alien, and the cure for AIDS.

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:37.399
You almost don't realize you're learning something.

00:00:37.399 --> 00:00:38.890
He Shows His Flaws

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:42.570
The hardest part of being relatable is letting
yourself be a little vulnerable.

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:47.290
Chappelle is a master of reminding us that
he's just another guy—wildly successful,

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:49.780
but just as insecure as anybody else.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:53.350
And he's not afraid to admit when he's made
a mistake, or at least a bad joke.

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:57.520
The infamous Wayne Brady incident is a perfect
example, with a joke about Brady's supposed

00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:59.180
whiteness going too far.

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:07.939
“White people love Wayne Brady because he
makes Bryant Gumbel look like Malcom X.”

00:01:07.939 --> 00:01:10.200
Chappelle brought Brady on the show as an
apology.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:13.990
The resulting sketch about how Brady was too
street even for Chappelle became an instant

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:14.990
classic.

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:19.939
And all because Chappelle was open enough
to realize and apologize for his mistake.

00:01:19.939 --> 00:01:20.999
Hip-Hop Ambassador

00:01:20.999 --> 00:01:24.399
The show's relationship with music brought
some of the all-time greats into homes of

00:01:24.399 --> 00:01:25.969
millions of Americans.

00:01:25.969 --> 00:01:29.779
But it's the movie Dave Chappelle's Block
Party that really proves his hip-hop and soul

00:01:29.779 --> 00:01:31.039
bona fides.

00:01:31.039 --> 00:01:34.509
The movie is a behind-the-scenes look at the
days leading up to a free block party Dave

00:01:34.509 --> 00:01:36.229
threw on the streets of New York.

00:01:36.229 --> 00:01:39.280
Its inclusiveness is heartwarming, and joy
radiates from every frame.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:41.859
“Next thing we know we see Dave Chappelle.

00:01:41.859 --> 00:01:42.859
“

00:01:42.859 --> 00:01:43.859
“Ha Ha Ha…

00:01:43.859 --> 00:01:45.060
Do you like rap music?

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:47.459
WIll y’all come to my block party in New
York?

00:01:47.459 --> 00:01:49.459
It’s in Brooklyn on Saturday”

00:01:49.459 --> 00:01:50.459
“Ohhh”

00:01:50.459 --> 00:01:53.679
It's the rare concert film that feels truly
special, as the show goes on in the pouring

00:01:53.679 --> 00:01:57.539
rain in front of an audience full of people
who otherwise might not be able to afford

00:01:57.539 --> 00:01:59.229
the concert's fantastic lineup.

00:01:59.229 --> 00:02:02.049
Oh, and there's some jokes, too.

00:02:02.049 --> 00:02:03.239
Master Of The Craft

00:02:03.239 --> 00:02:07.349
Dave famously got his start in stand-up at
a young age, climbing onstage at age 14 and

00:02:07.349 --> 00:02:08.520
just riffing on whatever.

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:13.069
Sure, he has constructed jokes, but he's comfortable
fitting them in between bits of stream-of-consciousness

00:02:13.069 --> 00:02:15.280
comedy and audience interaction.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:18.829
Sometimes it backfires, and his comedy turns
downright antagonistic.

00:02:18.829 --> 00:02:22.370
But even when it's not funny, there's something
thrilling about seeing a master in the process

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:24.099
of creating.

00:02:24.099 --> 00:02:25.780
Frank And Inclusive On Race

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:29.519
If there's one thing about Chappelle's comedy
that has true importance, it's the frank way

00:02:29.519 --> 00:02:33.549
in which he talks about race, using humor
to bridge the gaps between people.

00:02:33.549 --> 00:02:37.549
Take, for example, the classic sketch "I Know
Black People," in which Chappelle irreverently

00:02:37.549 --> 00:02:41.470
explores the inherent humor in our differences
by testing random people on their knowledge

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:43.030
of black culture.

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:47.040
It's these little observations, surface-funny
but deeply felt, that truly do a service to

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:48.269
society at large.

00:02:48.269 --> 00:02:52.090
We're different, but we're not that different,
and none of us are too good to be the butt

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:53.379
of a great joke.

00:02:53.379 --> 00:02:56.890
It's an approach that brings us closer, and
it's why—even all these years after he edged

00:02:56.890 --> 00:02:59.810
out of the spotlight—we're still waiting
for Dave Chappelle.

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:01.040
Thanks for watching!

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:04.500
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:07.469
And leave us a comment to let us know what
your favorite Dave Chappelle sketch is…

